The problem requires us to divide the plane into regions based on the number of triangles. We can start by dividing the plane into two regions, one for each triangle. Each region can then be subdivided further based on the number of triangles it contains. We can recursively apply this process until we reach a stopping condition, where each triangle is assigned to a single region.

In this solution, we start by dividing the plane into two regions, each for a single triangle. We then recursively subdivide each region based on the number of triangles it contains. The stopping condition is reached when each triangle is assigned to a single region. The maximum number of regions is therefore the number of triangles plus one, as each triangle is assigned to a single region.

public static int maxRegions(int N) {
  // Assign each triangle to a region
  int[] regions = new int[N];
  // For each triangle, add 1 to the region
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    regions[i] = i % 2 == 0 ? 1 : 2;
  }
  // Merge regions with odd and even numbers
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (regions[i] % 2 != 0) {
      for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
        if (regions[j] % 2 == 0) {
          regions[i] = regions[j];
          break;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Count the number of unique regions
  int uniqueRegions = 0;
  for (int region : regions) {
    if (region != 0) {
      uniqueRegions++;
    }
  }
  return uniqueRegions;
}

